 "claim": "Arc signals and repeaters. After a frozen hop an opened handle's relay arrives at the next 3-edge cut as an arc signal: two ports fixed and disagreeing, the third free. (1) The arc lemma: an arc signal on ports a and b freezes a line exactly when the line has a corner on the own arc between a and b. Sufficiency is proved from the reading lemma (both classes bar that arc, and z_a fixes its points); the converse holds on a ladder, a prism, the 16- and 64-point grids, a grown space and a thick tube (160 of 160 on-arc cases frozen, 0 of 3,542 off-arc cases), with 32 junction exceptions on a ladder arm spliced onto a grid. So a station passes an arc signal on exactly when its out-line has a corner on the signal's arc. (2) Repeaters (validated pipeline): along an aligned chain of three strip stations with separated cuts, every ring point's signal leaves every station unchanged (32, 16 or 0 live pins at each cut); along a misaligned chain no live pin survives past the first station. (3) Depth: in 21 chain configurations (strips, 16-point grids and cubes, every out-cut placement), wherever a station's cuts are separated every creation k cuts from the ring passed cut k as a live signal; aligned configurations carry the relay four cuts into the final grid, misaligned ones stop within two; a live signal reaching the final grid creates there in 20 of 44 cases, as creation also needs the claim rule. Where a station's two cuts are three steps apart a line meets both, and a relay can pass a lost signal.",
